;;; A relation of the program's own left unguessed because a module it
;;; imports for another name loads an obligation rule — **W97**, register
;;; row `W97/symmetric-import` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.algebra` imported for `symmetric`, which makes `near` symmetric,
;;; beside `likes`, a relation the program declares and nothing closes, over
;;; two things. The program names no obligation and activates none; the
;;; import loads `std.algebra`'s two obligation rules with the module. The
;;; control, `rung-symmetric-written.ein`, deletes the import and writes the
;;; one rule it used, `std.algebra`'s `symmetric`, in its place.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No hrule, and no obligation activated at root, so the blind enumerator
;;; builds H: `(likes A B)`, `(likes B A)`, `(near A B)` and `(near B A)`.
;;; Nothing refutes any of them, and `symmetric` makes each `near` fact bring
;;; the other: R is 16 commitments and 8 states, the control's answer.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No candidate, and R is {∅}, one state: the loaded obligation rules take
;;; the ladder's second rung, which owes nothing and proposes nothing. The
;;; read-out names `near` as outside the universe and says nothing of
;;; `likes`, which no rule concludes and no query asks about.

(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric))
(relation near Thing Thing :why "{?1} is near {?2}")
(relation likes Thing Thing :why "{?1} likes {?2}")
(thing A) (thing B)
(symmetric near)
(solve)
